module gunbc.rung_drop.floor_cut_fmt_gate

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.rung_drop { RungDrop, Standing }

data floor_cut_fmt_gate: RungDrop = RungDrop {
  identity: "floor_cut_fmt_gate" as NonEmptyStr,

  subject: "Rust fmt as a merge-path gate",

  declared: "2026-09-01",

  standing: Standing,

  authored: "**NO REQUIRED LANE RUNS `cargo fmt`, AND THIS ROW DECLARES THAT RUNG (2026-09-01).** Split out of `floor_cut`. PREVIOUS RUNG: 2 (mechanically preventable) -- fmt ran as a required CI step on the merge path. TEMPORARY RUNG: 1 (mitigatable) and generously so: what remains is the generated pre-commit hook, which is opt-in per clone (`core.hooksPath`), bypassable with `--no-verify`, and absent in container worktrees. REASON: the 2026-08-15 floor cut deleted the build job that carried the step. BOUNDED POPULATION: one capability -- refusal of unformatted Rust at the merge boundary. MEASURED FROM THE TREE (2026-09-01): the emitted `.github/workflows/witnesses.yml` runs fmt in neither required lane. This is not a new reading -- `gunbc.commit_workflow` already carries it at check grain as an UNCOVERED residual in `check_removal_roster`, and records the sharper fact that the hook's own slimming was argued FROM fmt remaining a required-path CI step, which the cut then falsified. `gunbc.repo_self_build` names the command; nothing required executes it. PRIOR EVIDENCE THE HOOK IS NOT AN AUTHORITY, from that same carrier: gunbc#6658 -- an unformatted `.rs` landed on main and no hook caught it. RESTORATION TRIGGER, A CAPABILITY: `cargo fmt --all --check` executing inside a required lane such that a merge with unformatted Rust is REFUSED, demonstrated by a red on an unformatted fixture. WHAT DOES NOT RETIRE THIS ROW: any strengthening of the pre-commit or pre-push hook -- that surface was proven non-enforcing before this drop was declared, and re-heavying it re-imports the pressure `commit_workflow` records as converting honest reds into workaround greens. **`floor_cut` CANNOT RETIRE WHILE THIS ROW STANDS**, and this sentence is on every one of the five so the constraint is readable from either end: that row's trigger is the conjunction of these five, so retiring it on a reading of any single sibling -- including this one -- is the reading its own trigger forbids."
}
